{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01QRxX89Kppj4mvwbgHMDUK8","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Let me analyze the project context carefully and propose the right task.\n\n## Project Summary\n\n**Problem**: go-unix-utils is an experiment in specification-driven development using classic Unix utilities as the test bed. It derives formal specifications from C source code, generates Go implementations from those specs using an AI coding agent, and verifies parity against reference binaries through differential testing.\n\n**Architecture**: Four-stage pipeline (extract, specify, synthesize, verify) with:\n- `cmd/` - Go implementations of each utility\n- `pkg/sys/` - Darwin/Linux syscall abstractions\n- `pkg/format/` - Shared output formatting\n\n\n- `pkg/testutils/` - Differential testing harness\n- `magefiles/` - Build targets\n- `docs/` - Specifications\n\n**Current State**: I have working implementations for pkg/testutils, pkg/sys, pkg/format, and several utilities (cat, du, wc, sponge, true, version), though the release tracking shows some inconsistency about what's actually complete versus what exists in the codebase.\n\n**Releases**: rel01.0 through rel01.3 all have specs done but show code as not started, even though the source files are present for wc, cat, sponge, and du. rel02.0 is pending with true already implemented, while rel02.1 and rel02.2 (tee and head) haven't been started yet.\n\n**cmd/true status**: Issue #204 is already tracking the implementation of cmd/true with a \"ready\" status, so I shouldn't propose it again. The source code shows cmd/true/main.go exists but there's no test file for it yet.\n\n**Reconciling code vs. status**: The source code section contains .go files for multiple packages that show \"not started\" in the code_status, which suggests the code was generated but the tracking hasn't been updated. Based on the constraint to trust source code over documentation, I should treat existing .go files as already implemented and focus on what's actually missing.\n\n**Identifying what to propose**: Looking at the existing packages in source_code (pkg/testutils, pkg/sys, pkg/format, cmd/cat, cmd/du, cmd/wc, cmd/sponge), I need to find gaps where either the implementation is incomplete or tests are missing, rather than proposing packages that already have code.\n\n**Handling the orphaned PRDs**: The PRDs for true and false exist but are marked as orphaned, which means they're disconnected from the roadmap structure. For rel02.0, I should check whether the existing prd020-true and prd021-false can be properly linked, or if new PRDs need to be created for the other rel02.0 commands (basename, dirname) that don't have any PRD yet.\n\n**Clarifying cmd/true's status**: Since cmd/true already has implementation code in main.go and issue #204 is marked \"ready,\" I need to determine whether that issue covers the full implementation including tests, or if it's incomplete. If the implementation is already done, I shouldn't propose it again—but if tests are missing, that might be a separate concern.\n\n**Next step for cmd/false**: The prd021-false exists as an orphaned PRD, but there's no source code or issue for cmd/false yet, so this would be a legitimate new implementation to propose without duplicating existing work. says \"Implement cmd/true (prd020-true)\" which covers implementation, not tests. Since cmd/true/main.go already exists and references the PRD requirements, the logical next task is writing tests for it—following the principle that implementation and tests should be separate tasks. I should also check what the earliest incomplete release is to prioritize correctly. Looking at rel02.0, true has implementation but needs tests, false has a PRD but no code yet, and yes/basename/dirname are all blocked waiting for their PRDs to be written first. Since issue #204 covers the true implementation that's already done, the next priority should be getting tests in place for true and then tackling false's implementation. or was already completed before the issue was created. Either way, I should focus on proposing work that's in the earliest incomplete release, doesn't duplicate existing issues, and has its prerequisites met. The clearest task would be implementing differential tests for cmd/true across the R1-R5 test ranges, since the implementation already exists and the testing infrastructure is in place. Let me look at what prd020-true actually specifies to write a proper task description.\n\nLooking at the requirements, I can see the test cases cover basic behavior like silent exit on no arguments, ignoring extra arguments, handling --help and --version flags, and error cases. The tests should follow the same differential testing pattern used in other command tests, comparing against the GNU reference binary. I'm trying to verify whether Homebrew's coreutils package actually installs true as gtrue...\n\nyes, it does, so that's the reference binary I'll use for the tests.\n\nNow I'm laying out the test structure for the true command—I need differential tests that compare our implementation against GNU's version across several key scenarios: default behavior with and without arguments, help and version flags in different positions, and error handling for write failures. I'll follow the existing pattern from the wc tests and use the DiffTest harness to keep things consistent. test.go and cmd/cat/cat_test.go\"\n      - id: D2\n        text: \"Reference binary is gtrue from Homebrew coreutils, located via exec.LookPath\"\n      - id: D3\n        text: \"TestMain builds the Go true binary to a temp directory and locates gtrue; skips all tests if gtrue is not found\"\n      - id: D4\n        text: \"Use LC_ALL=C environment via pkg/testutils default behavior (ARCHITECTURE.yaml DD6)\"\n\n    acceptance_criteria:\n      - id: AC1\n        text: \"cmd/true/true_test.go compiles with no errors\"\n      - id: AC2\n        text: \"go test ./cmd/true/ passes when gtrue is available on PATH\"\n      - id: AC3\n        text: \"Tests cover R1 (exit 0 silently), R2 (--help, --version), R3 (argument ignored), R5 (exit codes)\"\n      - id: AC4\n        text: \"Test file follows the project differential testing pattern: TestMain builds binary, tests use testutils.RunDiffTests\"\n\nI'm realizing there's a complication with how `true` handles `--help` and `--version` flags. The GNU version prints help or version info and exits cleanly, but our Go implementation uses a hardcoded version string that won't match what gtrue outputs. This means those flags will always show differences between the two binaries, so I'll need to set up normalizers to handle the divergent output for those specific cases.\n\nLooking at how other utilities in the project handle this, the test files for wc, cat, and du don't actually test `--help` and `--version` at all. But since prd020-true explicitly requires testing those flags, I need to figure out how to make the differential tests work despite the output differences. The core challenge is that `true` is fundamentally different from other utilities—its only real behavior is returning exit code 0, and the `--help`/`--version` flags are edge cases where output text will inevitably diverge.\n\nThe practical solution is to focus differential testing on what actually matters: verifying that various argument combinations all produce exit code 0, and that unrecognized flags are silently ignored. For `--help` and `--version` specifically, I should skip the output comparison and only verify the exit codes match, since the help text and version strings will naturally differ between implementations. I'm overthinking this—the stitch agent will read the PRD and determine the right testing strategy. I should just document that --help/--version output will naturally diverge from the reference binary and move forward. For `true` specifically, since its core behavior is just exiting with code 0, a hybrid approach makes sense: differential tests for the fundamental behavior and unit tests for --help/--version where the output text would inherently differ.\n\nNow I need to anchor this to the PRD requirements properly. Looking at what I can infer from the source code, I've got the core behaviors mapped out: unconditional exit 0, --help and --version handling, and argument handling. The constraint is 4 requirements maximum per task, so I need to structure these carefully and pick the most critical ones to focus on. I need to map out the specific test requirements for the true command, covering default behavior, help and version flags, and ensuring those flags only work as the first argument. For --help and --version flags, the output will differ between the Go and GNU implementations, so I'll use a normalization function to strip stdout and compare only exit codes and stderr. The acceptance criteria cover compilation, test execution when gtrue is available, comprehensive test coverage for the true command's behavior, and adherence to the project's differential testing pattern.\n\nI'm reconsidering whether D4 is too prescriptive—the key insight is that naively testing --help and --version with RunDiffTests will fail due to stdout divergence, so the normalizer approach cleanly handles this by zeroing out stdout for those specific cases. For the true command's simplicity, I should focus on verifying exit code 0 across various argument patterns through differential testing, while handling --help and --version separately by checking only their exit codes. difference? GNU true's `--help` exits 0, Go true's `--help` exits 0. GNU true's `--version` exits 0, Go true's `--version` exits 0. The exit codes match. The only divergence is the stdout content.\n\nActually, I just realized something. Does GNU `gtrue` even support `--help` and `--version`? Let me think... Yes, GNU coreutils `true` does support `--help` and `--version`. From the GNU coreutils source:\n- `true --help` prints help and exits EXIT_SUCCESS (0)\n- `true --version` prints version info and exits EXIT_SUCCESS (0)\n\nSo both the Go and GNU versions exit 0 for --help/--version. The divergence is only in stdout text.\n\nFor `--help` and `--version`, I probably shouldn't be comparing stdout at all since the help text will naturally differ between implementations. I could either skip those tests entirely or write a separate helper that only validates exit codes. The pragmatic approach is to just compare exit codes for these flags and ignore the output differences.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":87529,"cache_read_input_tokens":19289,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":87529},"output_tokens":10,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5945aad8-bf9b-4619-be47-c62a53c6f4b9","uuid":"1a98e22e-596b-4a32-a13e-cb4d98569215"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01QRxX89Kppj4mvwbgHMDUK8","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"## Project State Summary\n\n**Problem**: go-unix-utils is a specification-driven experiment that derives formal specs from C source code of GNU utilities, generates Go implementations via AI, and verifies parity through differential testing against Homebrew reference binaries.\n\n**Architecture**: Four-stage pipeline (extract → specify → synthesize → verify). Key components: `cmd/` (per-utility Go implementations), `pkg/testutils/` (differential testing harness), `pkg/sys/` (platform syscall wrappers), `pkg/format/` (shared formatting), `magefiles/` (build targets, scaffolded externally).\n\n**Current implementation state** (from source_code, which overrides prose):\n- **Complete with tests**: pkg/testutils, pkg/sys, pkg/format, cmd/wc, cmd/cat, cmd/sponge, cmd/du, cmd/version\n- **Implementation exists, no tests**: cmd/true (main.go exists, no true_test.go)\n- **Not started**: cmd/false, cmd/yes, cmd/basename, cmd/dirname, cmd/tee, cmd/head\n\n**Current release priority**: Releases 00.0 through 01.3 have source code for all components. Release 02.0 is the earliest incomplete release. Within 02.0, cmd/true has implementation but no test file. Issue #204 covers implementation (already done). PRDs exist for true (prd020-true) and false (prd021-false, orphaned).\n\n## Priority Reasoning\n\nThe earliest incomplete work is **cmd/true tests** in rel02.0. The implementation exists (cmd/true/main.go, referencing prd020-true R1-R5), but no test file exists. Per P2, tests are always a separate task from implementation. Issue #204 covers implementation (not tests), so this is not a duplicate. Dependencies are met: pkg/testutils exists, cmd/true/main.go exists.\n\n```yaml\n- index: 0\n  title: \"Implement cmd/true differential tests (prd020-true R1-R5)\"\n  dependency: -1\n  description: |\n    deliverable_type: code\n\n    required_reading:\n      - docs/specs/product-requirements/prd020-true.yaml (requirements R1-R5 for true utility)\n      - cmd/true/main.go (existing implementation to verify against reference binary)\n      - pkg/testutils/difftest.go (DiffTest struct and RunDiffTests harness API)\n      - cmd/cat/cat_test.go (reference pattern for differential test file structure)\n\n    files:\n      - path: cmd/true/true_test.go\n        action: create\n        note: Differential tests for cmd/true against GNU reference binary gtrue\n\n    requirements:\n      - id: R1\n        text: \"Test default exit behavior: no arguments exits 0, arbitrary arguments are silently ignored and still exit 0 (prd020-true R1, R3)\"\n      - id: R2\n        text: \"Test --help and --version as first argument each produce exit 0 with stdout output (prd020-true R2)\"\n      - id: R3\n        text: \"Test that --help and --version are only recognized as the first argument -- when passed as second or later arguments, true still exits 0 with no stdout (prd020-true R2.3)\"\n      - id: R4\n        text: \"Test exit code parity between Go binary and reference binary for all argument combinations (prd020-true R5)\"\n\n    design_decisions:\n      - id: D1\n        text: \"Use pkg/testutils.RunDiffTests for core behavior tests, following the pattern in cmd/cat/cat_test.go: TestMain builds the Go binary to a temp directory and locates the reference binary via exec.LookPath\"\n      - id: D2\n        text: \"Reference binary is gtrue from Homebrew coreutils, located via exec.LookPath(\\\"gtrue\\\") in TestMain; skip all tests gracefully if gtrue is not found on PATH\"\n      - id: D3\n        text: \"For --help and --version tests, stdout text will diverge between Go and GNU binaries (different version strings and help text). Use a NormalizeFunc that replaces all stdout content with empty bytes so that only exit code and stderr are compared for these specific test cases\"\n\n    acceptance_criteria:\n      - id: AC1\n        text: \"cmd/true/true_test.go compiles with no errors via go build ./cmd/true/\"\n      - id: AC2\n        text: \"go test ./cmd/true/ passes when gtrue is available on PATH\"\n      - id: AC3\n        text: \"Tests cover: no-args exit 0, single arbitrary arg exit 0, multiple arbitrary args exit 0, --help as first arg exit 0, --version as first arg exit 0, --help as non-first arg silently ignored\"\n      - id: AC4\n        text: \"Test file follows the project differential testing pattern: TestMain builds binary and locates reference, tests use testutils.RunDiffTests, LC_ALL=C is applied by the harness default\"\n```"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":87529,"cache_read_input_tokens":19289,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":87529},"output_tokens":10,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5945aad8-bf9b-4619-be47-c62a53c6f4b9","uuid":"8511f69a-216a-4416-8bfd-694b89ae86ab"}
